{slug:'task-contract',comp:'m1.c1',title:'Specify a task, evidence boundary and response contract',before:'Complete Language Model Fundamentals. Review application authority, context and supported API message roles.',intro:'A university help center asks an assistant to summarize a supplied policy. A useful prompt says what to do, what evidence to use, what to return and what to do when the policy does not answer the question.',sections:[S('Turn vague intent into an observable task','Specify the requested operation, audience, relevant constraints and output contract. For a policy summary, distinguish extracting supplied facts from generating general advice. Include a truthful insufficient-evidence path rather than forcing the model to invent missing dates or exceptions.','Separate application-controlled instructions from user content and retrieved documents. Message roles and delimiters help express structure, but neither makes untrusted text harmless nor grants database permissions. The application must still authorize source retrieval and downstream actions.'),S('Make constraints testable','A prompt such as be helpful is difficult to evaluate. A task contract such as return three concise points supported by the supplied policy, preserve exceptions, and state unknown when evidence is absent gives reviewers observable properties to check. Avoid contradictory instructions that require both exhaustive detail and an impossibly tiny output.','Record a task-template version together with the selected model and adapter capabilities. Exact role names and priority rules are provider-specific; the fixture builds a synthetic internal message representation, not a universal API payload.')],problem:'Construct a synthetic task request with distinct trusted instructions and user material.',code:'import json\ntask = "Summarize supplied policy; preserve exceptions; say unknown when unsupported."\nuser = {"question": "When can I register?", "policy": "Registration opens Monday; late entry requires approval."}\nmessages = [{"role": "system", "content": task}, {"role": "user", "content": json.dumps(user, sort_keys=True)}]\nprint([m["role"] for m in messages])\nprint("late entry" in messages[1]["content"])\nprint(messages[0]["content"] == task)',expect:"['system', 'user']\nTrue\nTrue",steps:['Write the task contract separately from supplied material.','Serialize the user question and policy as data.','Inspect the internal role separation and retained exception.'],limit:'This fixture checks local message construction only. It does not prove prompt-injection resistance, provider role compatibility or generated answer quality.',mistakes:['Using a vague instruction with no observable acceptance criteria.','Treating delimiters or roles as substitutes for application authorization.'],takeaways:['State the task and evidence limits explicitly.','Evaluate instructions as a versioned behavioral contract.'],refs:['structure','tokens','evals'],questions:[
['What makes a prompt constraint useful for evaluation?','An observable requirement','Only a friendly tone','Only a longer message','Only a model nickname','A testable requirement lets reviewers judge whether the output meets the intended task.','Tone alone leaves task acceptance vague.','Length does not establish clarity.','Names do not define behavior.'],
['What should happen when required policy evidence is absent?','Use the unknown path','Invent a likely rule','Treat confidence as authority','Change the policy silently','A task contract should define truthful behavior for unsupported requests.','Guesses are not supplied evidence.','Confidence does not add support.','Generation does not update policy.'],
['Do delimiters around source text grant permission to access it?','No, access is separate','Yes, for any private document','Yes, if the text is short','Yes, when JSON parses','Structure can clarify content but cannot replace application data authorization.','Private access still needs permission.','Length does not authorize disclosure.','Syntax is not an access grant.'],
['A support prompt says both preserve every exception and return exactly one word. What needs review?','Conflicting task constraints','Only the browser’s theme','Only the provider request id','Only dictionary key sorting','Contradictory constraints can make the intended output impossible or ambiguous.','Theme does not resolve instructions.','Ids do not establish feasible behavior.','Sorting cannot reconcile the task.'],
['A pasted policy contains commands to change system behavior. What should the application retain?','Its trusted task boundary','The document’s claimed admin role','Every embedded command as policy','Unrestricted private retrieval','Source material is evidence input rather than authority to redefine the application’s feature.','Claims inside data do not authenticate roles.','Embedded instructions are untrusted content.','Retrieval remains scoped.'],
['A team changes task instructions without recording a version. What becomes harder?','Attributing behavior changes','Seeing a displayed answer','Counting stored messages','Rendering plain text','Version metadata helps explain which instructions produced a result.','Visibility can still be observed.','Storage counts are separate.','Rendering does not depend on version attribution.'],
['The fixture preserves a policy exception in its serialized input. What remains unproved?','The model will preserve it','The text contains the exception','The roles are separate strings','The input can be JSON-serialized','Local payload construction does not measure actual generated behavior.','The fixture directly checks presence.','The list explicitly contains the roles.','Serialization is executed.'],
['A reviewer wants to improve a vague policy-summary task. Which addition helps most?','Explicit support and format rules','Only more enthusiastic wording','Only a larger prompt title','Only a new assistant avatar','Concrete evidence and output requirements make the task clearer and testable.','Enthusiasm does not specify acceptance.','Titles do not define constraints.','Avatars do not govern reasoning.']
]},
{slug:'examples',comp:'m1.c1',title:'Use examples to clarify a task without leaking its test answers',before:'Use the task-contract lesson. Distinguish input examples from independent evaluation cases.',intro:'A customer-support classifier must label messages as billing, delivery or other. A few carefully chosen demonstrations can clarify boundary cases, but repeated near-identical examples can hide an unclear task definition.',sections:[S('Choose examples for coverage, not decoration','Few-shot examples show input/output pairs that illustrate the required transformation. Include representative cases, important boundaries and a consistent schema. Examples should agree with the written rules; a contradictory demonstration can teach the wrong pattern.','Do not include private information without permission or place the final evaluation answers into the prompt accidentally. Demonstrations are part of the model’s input for that request, not evidence that its weights have been retrained.'),S('Check consistency and test transfer','A duplicate input with conflicting labels is a useful warning that the rubric or data need repair. Superficial variants of the same easy example do not establish general coverage. Evaluate unseen cases, ambiguous cases and cases requiring the unknown/other path.','The fixture finds a contradictory synthetic demonstration by exact input key. It is not semantic duplicate detection, a model classifier or proof that the remaining examples are sufficient. Human review should also check category definitions and business consequences of misrouting.')],problem:'Detect a conflicting label in a synthetic few-shot demonstration set.',code:'examples = [("Where is my parcel?", "delivery"), ("I was charged twice", "billing"), ("Where is my parcel?", "billing")]\nseen = {}\nconflicts = []\nfor text, label in examples:\n    if text in seen and seen[text] != label:\n        conflicts.append(text)\n    seen[text] = label\nprint(len(conflicts))\nprint(conflicts[0])',expect:'1\nWhere is my parcel?',steps:['Read input/output demonstrations as task evidence.','Compare repeated exact inputs for inconsistent labels.','Surface the conflict before using the prompt.'],limit:'Exact-key comparison misses paraphrases and does not establish the correct business label. No model prediction or generalization test occurs.',mistakes:['Using contradictory examples without resolving the labeling rule.','Treating repeated name-swapped cases as broad evaluation coverage.'],takeaways:['Demonstrations should align with an explicit rubric.','Keep independent tests separate from prompt examples.'],refs:['evals','tokens'],questions:[
['What is the primary role of a few-shot demonstration?','Show the requested transformation','Grant account privileges','Update weights automatically','Replace every written constraint','Examples illustrate the intended behavior as request context.','Examples do not authorize users.','In-context input is not necessarily training.','Rules can still be necessary.'],
['What does conflicting labeling of the same input suggest?','The task data need review','The model is guaranteed correct','The output schema is encrypted','The test set is independent','A contradiction can indicate an unclear rubric or an erroneous demonstration.','Conflicts do not establish quality.','Labels do not encrypt schemas.','Independence is a separate property.'],
['Should final evaluation answers normally be included as prompt demonstrations?','No, preserve independent evidence','Yes, to prove generalization','Yes, if the test is hard','Yes, after renaming record ids','Using test answers in the prompt compromises the interpretation of independent evaluation.','Seeing answers is not unseen-case transfer.','Difficulty does not restore independence.','New ids do not remove content leakage.'],
['A support classifier has ten examples that differ only in customer names. What is weak?','Boundary-case coverage','Only JSON syntax','Only the displayed font','Only the model request id','Name substitutions may repeat one easy pattern while omitting meaningful task distinctions.','The examples may parse correctly.','Fonts do not create coverage.','Ids do not define task variety.'],
['An example contradicts the written delivery/billing rule. What should the author do?','Resolve the rubric conflict','Keep both to increase volume','Hide the written rule','Call the conflict random creativity','Examples and rules should express a coherent task before model evaluation.','More records do not fix contradiction.','Hiding rules obscures intent.','Classification labels need consistent meaning.'],
['A demonstration contains a real private invoice. What must govern its use?','Authorized minimal data','Only the example’s usefulness','Only a shorter prompt length','Only a low sampling setting','Educational or task value does not override privacy and authorized data scope.','Useful data can still be private.','Short disclosures remain disclosures.','Sampling does not grant access.'],
['The exact-duplicate fixture finds no conflicts. What remains unchecked?','Semantic label consistency','The exact input comparison','The local loop execution','The stored label strings','Different wording can still represent conflicting or duplicated task meaning.','The comparison is directly exercised.','The loop runs locally.','Labels are explicit data.'],
['A model follows the example schema on one new case. What is still needed?','Broader task evaluation','A claim of universal success','Deletion of all failure tests','Automatic production publication','One result is not enough to establish behavior across representative and critical cases.','Finite evidence is not universal proof.','Failures remain useful evidence.','Publication requires the actual workflow gates.']
]},
{slug:'context-management',comp:'m1.c2',title:'Manage conversation history with a task-aware budget',before:'Review context windows and token accounting. Keep required instructions separate from optional history.',intro:'A SaaS assistant’s conversation becomes long enough that sending every turn would exceed the model’s window. The system needs a deliberate strategy for what to preserve, summarize, retrieve or leave out.',sections:[S('Prioritize meaning before truncation','Reserve required instructions, the current user task, necessary evidence and an output allowance. Then select optional history according to relevance and freshness. Blindly cutting bytes or deleting all oldest turns can remove an important constraint or break a structured message.','Summaries can help but are transformations, not lossless memory. Preserve provenance, unresolved decisions and critical exceptions, and evaluate whether the summary remains sufficient for the next task. Avoid treating every old private detail as necessary forever.'),S('Make missing-context behavior visible','If a current request depends on omitted material, retrieve the authorized source or ask for clarification under the product policy. Do not claim the model remembers text that was not supplied. Keep model/encoding-specific token estimation separate from a rough character limit.','The fixture uses synthetic component counts and explicitly reserves current task/output before admitting optional history. It demonstrates accounting and selection, not a tokenizer, semantic retrieval model or optimal summarizer.')],problem:'Reserve required context, then select a relevant synthetic history item that fits.',code:'capacity = 600\nrequired = {"policy": 100, "current_question": 80, "output": 150}\nremaining = capacity - sum(required.values())\nitems = [("relevant_decision", 180), ("unrelated_chat", 160)]\nselected = []\nfor name, count in items:\n    if name == "relevant_decision" and count <= remaining:\n        selected.append(name)\n        remaining -= count\nprint(selected)\nprint(remaining)',expect:"['relevant_decision']\n90",steps:['Reserve the required task and answer capacity first.','Apply an explicit relevance decision to optional history.','Inspect the selected context and remaining allowance.'],limit:'The relevance labels and token counts are supplied synthetic data. This does not measure actual tokenization, decide real semantic relevance or guarantee that all necessary history was preserved.',mistakes:['Truncating structured context at arbitrary byte offsets.','Assuming an old summary is always complete and current.'],takeaways:['Preserve task-critical context before optional history.','Make context selection and omissions reviewable.'],refs:['tokens','evals'],questions:[
['Which material should be reserved before optional history?','Required task and output space','Every past greeting','All unrelated private records','Only the assistant’s last joke','A budget should preserve necessary instructions, current task/evidence and response capacity.','Greetings may be irrelevant.','Private records need purpose and access.','A joke is not a general priority.'],
['Why can arbitrary byte truncation be harmful?','It can break meaning or structure','It always lowers all prices','It guarantees no hallucination','It grants retrieval access','Byte cuts can damage structured input or remove important semantic boundaries.','Billing depends on actual usage and rates.','Less context can increase unsupported output.','Truncation does not authorize data.'],
['What must be evaluated about a generated history summary?','Required meaning','Only how short it is','Only its confident tone','Only its title length','Compression is useful only if the task-critical meaning and limitations are preserved.','Shortness alone can hide omissions.','Tone is not fidelity evidence.','Titles do not establish completeness.'],
['A user asks about a decision absent from supplied context. What should the app do?','Retrieve or clarify under policy','Pretend the model remembers it','Invent a plausible decision','Disclose all stored histories','The feature should acknowledge missing evidence and use an authorized recovery path.','Unsent text is not guaranteed context.','Plausibility is not a record.','Bulk disclosure crosses scope boundaries.'],
['A summary from last month contains an old shipping rule. What needs checking?','Source version and origin','Only the summary’s grammar','Only the number of bullets','Only the model’s brand','A summary can preserve stale information unless its source/version is tracked and refreshed appropriately.','Grammar does not establish applicability.','Bullets do not update policy.','Brand is not provenance.'],
['The fixture selects a relevant item using a hardcoded label. What does it not implement?','Semantic relevance detection','A local numeric comparison','A selected-item list','A fixed capacity calculation','The fixture assumes relevance labels and only demonstrates the downstream budget mechanics.','The comparison executes.','The list is constructed.','The arithmetic is directly performed.'],
['A team replays every private turn to improve continuity. Which additional concern applies?','Data purpose and retention','Only response punctuation','Only message bubble width','Only dictionary sorting','Continuity does not justify unnecessary sharing or indefinite retention of private content.','Punctuation is not a privacy policy.','Layout does not define data use.','Sorting does not establish purpose.'],
['A request fits a rough character cap. What still needs model-specific checking?','Actual token representation','The text field exists','The browser has a submit control','The local character count','Characters and model tokens are not interchangeable exact units.','Field presence is already locally observable.','UI controls do not define tokenization.','The count itself does not prove tokens fit.']
]},
{slug:'json-shape',comp:'m2.c1',title:'Validate JSON syntax and a narrow result schema',before:'Review Python JSON parsing and the difference between syntax, types and business validity.',intro:'A customer-support feature needs a category and priority, not a paragraph that merely looks like an object. Machine-readable output requires a clear schema and a safe failure path.',sections:[S('Syntax is the first gate, not the last','JSON parsing checks whether a representation follows JSON syntax. It does not enforce required properties, allowed keys, value types or business constraints. JSON mode and strict schema-constrained output features can differ; current support and schema subsets depend on the actual provider/model.','Define a stable application schema and validate at the consumption boundary even when a provider feature helps constrain output. Unknown fields should be rejected or handled under an explicit versioning policy, not silently copied into internal objects.'),S('Handle types precisely','A category enum constrains labels; an integer range constrains priority. In Python, bool is a subclass of int, so a naive isinstance(value, int) check may accept True where the contract requires a numeric priority. Choose checks that express the actual schema.','The fixture implements a small exact schema predicate after parsing. It is not a general JSON Schema engine, does not validate nested arbitrary structures and does not prove that the chosen category accurately describes the customer’s message.')],problem:'Parse synthetic output and enforce exact keys, enum and integer priority.',code:'import json\ndef valid(value):\n    return isinstance(value, dict) and set(value) == {"category", "priority"} and value["category"] in {"billing", "delivery", "other"} and type(value["priority"]) is int and 1 <= value["priority"] <= 3\nprint(valid(json.loads(\'{"category":"billing","priority":2}\')))\nprint(valid({"category": "billing", "priority": True}))\nprint(valid({"category": "billing", "priority": 2, "approved": True}))',expect:'True\nFalse\nFalse',steps:['Parse a valid synthetic JSON representation.','Check required/allowed fields and exact value types.','Reject a boolean-as-integer and an unmanaged field.'],limit:'This bounded predicate is intentionally not a full schema implementation or semantic classifier. Real integrations must also handle parser errors, refusal/incomplete states and provider capability differences.',mistakes:['Treating parseable JSON as proof of schema or semantic correctness.','Allowing unmanaged generated properties into internal models.'],takeaways:['Separate syntax, schema and business validation.','Test edge types and unknown fields explicitly.'],refs:['structure'],questions:[
['What does successful JSON parsing establish?','Syntax validity','Correct business meaning','User authorization','Current source freshness','Parsing establishes a representation’s syntax, leaving schema and semantic checks separate.','Meaning needs task validation.','Syntax does not grant access.','Parsing does not update evidence.'],
['What does an enum in a result schema constrain?','Allowed value labels','The truth of every label','The caller’s account role','The model’s training data','An enum restricts the representation to a defined set of values.','A permitted label can still be wrong.','Authorization is external policy.','Schemas do not retrain models.'],
['Why does the fixture use type(priority) is int?','Exclude boolean values','Encrypt the priority field','Make every number valid','Bypass output validation','Python booleans can pass a broad integer isinstance check, so the predicate states the narrower contract.','Type checking is not encryption.','The range still matters.','This is part of validation.'],
['A generated object contains an extra approved field. What should a narrow schema do?','Reject unmanaged properties','Copy it into the database','Treat it as administrator approval','Hide it only with CSS','Unknown managed fields should not silently gain authority in downstream state.','Copying can enable overposting.','Generated text does not grant approval.','Hidden data still reaches the client.'],
['A strict provider output mode is enabled. What still needs application handling?','Refusal and incomplete outcomes','A universal truth guarantee','Automatic object ownership','Unlimited output size','Provider schema assistance does not remove response-lifecycle and application validation responsibilities.','Shape constraints do not prove facts.','Ownership remains server policy.','Resource bounds still apply.'],
['A schema-valid category is incorrect for the customer’s actual request. Which layer failed?','Task semantic quality','Only JSON parsing','Only the enum membership','Only the exact-key predicate','A value can satisfy shape constraints while misrepresenting the task.','Syntax may be valid.','The label may be permitted.','The object may have the correct keys.'],
['A model version supports only a subset of schema features. What should the adapter check?','Actual schema capabilities','Only whether Python prints the schema','Only a copied old example','Only the UI’s loading state','Compatibility must be established for the actual provider/model contract.','Printing is not remote acceptance.','Historical examples can become stale.','UI state does not define schema support.'],
['Malformed JSON arrives from a completed response. What should consumption do?','Use an explicit validation failure','Invent missing fields silently','Execute the raw text as code','Mark the action successful','Invalid representations need a controlled failure/recovery policy rather than fabricated state.','Silent invention hides invalid input.','Generated text is not executable authority.','No valid action is established.']
]},
{slug:'semantic-checks',comp:'m2.c1',title:'Check business meaning after the schema passes',before:'Use the JSON-shape lesson and server authorization principles. A valid representation can still be wrong.',intro:'An invoice assistant extracts line totals into a structured record. Every field can have the right type while the totals disagree or the invoice belongs to another customer.',sections:[S('Express domain invariants explicitly','Schema checks define shape; domain checks define relationships such as subtotal plus tax equals total, a date falls in an allowed interval or a resource belongs to the current tenant. Keep authoritative values from trusted records rather than assuming the model’s repeated numbers are correct.','For monetary examples, use declared decimal units and a clear rounding policy. An arithmetic consistency check can detect one class of error but cannot prove an invoice is genuine or that every line was extracted correctly.'),S('Validate at the operation boundary','If structured output will cause a write or decision, recheck access, current state and writable fields when that action executes. A stale but previously valid proposal may no longer satisfy the business rule. Model output is proposed data, not a permission grant.','Return useful validation categories without exposing private internals. A repair/retry strategy should be bounded and should not let the model rewrite authoritative facts to make its own output pass. The fixture compares synthetic totals and owner scope only.')],problem:'Validate synthetic invoice arithmetic and owner scope with decimal amounts.',code:'from decimal import Decimal\ndef accept(actor, invoice):\n    return actor == invoice["owner"] and Decimal(invoice["subtotal"]) + Decimal(invoice["tax"]) == Decimal(invoice["total"])\na = {"owner": 7, "subtotal": "10.00", "tax": "1.50", "total": "11.50"}\nb = {**a, "total": "12.00"}\nprint(accept(7, a))\nprint(accept(7, b))\nprint(accept(8, a))',expect:'True\nFalse\nFalse',steps:['Use explicit decimal strings for the synthetic invoice.','Check the declared arithmetic relationship.','Require the trusted actor to match the permitted owner.'],limit:'The fixture assumes already validated decimal strings and checks two narrow conditions. It does not authenticate invoices, enforce atomic writes, handle every currency rule or prove extraction accuracy.',mistakes:['Accepting typed fields without cross-field invariants.','Letting generated owner identifiers replace trusted identity.'],takeaways:['A schema is necessary but not sufficient.','Keep domain authority outside model-generated values.'],refs:['structure','evals'],questions:[
['Which check goes beyond object shape?','A cross-field invariant','Only a required-key list','Only a string type test','Only enum membership','Domain relationships such as consistent totals require semantic validation beyond field structure.','Key presence is a shape check.','Type checks do not relate fields.','Enums constrain representation values.'],
['Why use trusted records for owner scope?','Generated ids are not authority','Every model id is authenticated','JSON grants cross-tenant access','A repeated field proves identity','Authorization must derive from trusted application state, not proposed output.','Generation does not authenticate ids.','Syntax does not grant access.','Repetition is not identity evidence.'],
['What does matching subtotal plus tax to total prove in the fixture?','That declared arithmetic agrees','The invoice is genuine','Every line was extracted correctly','The payment completed','The check establishes one numeric relationship under supplied assumptions.','Authenticity is not tested.','Other extraction errors can remain.','No payment operation occurs.'],
['An extraction has valid types but a contradictory total. What should the app do?','Reject or resolve the inconsistency','Accept because JSON is valid','Trust the model’s confident prose','Change trusted records to fit','Business consistency must be checked separately from shape and confidence.','Syntax does not settle arithmetic.','Tone does not prove numbers.','Authority should not be rewritten to fit output.'],
['A proposal was valid before an order shipped, but executes afterward. What should be checked?','Current state at execution','Only the old prompt snapshot','Only schema version spelling','Only response punctuation','State-dependent rules must hold at the authoritative action boundary.','Earlier context can be stale.','Names do not enforce state.','Punctuation is unrelated.'],
['A repair prompt changes the invoice’s stored tax amount merely to make its generated total pass. What boundary is wrong?','Misplaced data authority','Only token normalization','Only the browser layout','Only JSON escaping','Generated repair should not rewrite trusted source facts to manufacture consistency.','Tokenization does not set financial authority.','Layout does not fix data lineage.','Escaping does not establish truth.'],
['A schema-valid invoice belongs to another tenant. What outcome fits?','Deny unauthorized use','Accept the well-formed object','Hide only the tenant label','Ask the model for permission','Shape-valid private data still require appropriate application access.','Structure does not authorize access.','Hiding labels does not change ownership.','Models cannot grant tenant rights.'],
['The decimal fixture passes. What remains untested?','Real extraction authenticity','The local addition result','The synthetic owner comparison','The boolean outcomes printed','The example does not inspect an original real invoice or establish its authenticity.','Addition is executed.','Ownership equality is checked.','The outcomes are observed.']
]},
{slug:'response-outcomes',comp:'m2.c1',title:'Handle refusal, incompleteness and versioned output contracts',before:'Use schema and semantic validation. Review streaming terminal states and bounded retries.',intro:'A document assistant sometimes refuses a request or reaches its output limit. The application must represent those outcomes instead of assuming every response contains the expected business object.',sections:[S('Model the response envelope before parsing the payload','Distinguish successful complete output from refusal, incomplete output, transport errors and validation failures under the actual API contract. A refusal may be represented outside the requested schema. A truncated object may be syntactically invalid or miss required fields.','Check the operation state and response envelope before treating content as a valid result. Do not fill missing required fields with plausible defaults that imply a completed decision. A retry or repair policy should be bounded, logged safely and appropriate to the failure category.'),S('Version schemas deliberately','An output contract is consumed by application code, so changes to required keys, enums or meanings need compatibility handling. Record schema/task version and validate against the intended version rather than mixing old and new fields silently.','The fixture routes synthetic envelope states into separate application outcomes. It intentionally does not infer provider field names or statuses; actual adapters must translate their documented contract and handle evolving capabilities.')],problem:'Route synthetic complete, refused and incomplete outcomes before consuming results.',code:'def route(envelope):\n    if envelope["state"] == "refused":\n        return "show_refusal"\n    if envelope["state"] != "complete":\n        return "not_a_result"\n    return "validate_payload"\nfor state in ["complete", "refused", "incomplete"]:\n    print(route({"state": state}))',expect:'validate_payload\nshow_refusal\nnot_a_result',steps:['Inspect the synthetic response state before payload use.','Keep refusal separate from successful business results.','Send only complete outputs to schema/semantic validation.'],limit:'The state names are an internal educational contract, not exact provider response fields. The fixture does not parse a real API response, validate payload content or establish retry safety.',mistakes:['Treating every provider response as a valid schema object.','Silently inventing fields for incomplete output.'],takeaways:['Handle lifecycle outcomes before business parsing.','Keep schema evolution and recovery explicit.'],refs:['structure','stream','retry'],questions:[
['What should happen before consuming a business payload?','Check response lifecycle state','Assume every call succeeded','Fill every missing field','Execute any content as code','The application must distinguish complete output from refusal, interruption and other failures.','Calls can fail or refuse.','Invented fields hide invalid results.','Generated content is not executable authority.'],
['Can a refusal be outside the requested output schema?','Yes, handle its envelope','No, every refusal is valid data','No, the app must fabricate data','No, refusals grant permission','Provider contracts can represent refusal separately, so the adapter needs an explicit path.','Refusal is not the requested business result.','Fabrication hides the actual outcome.','Refusal does not authorize actions.'],
['Why version an output schema?','Manage consumer compatibility','Guarantee answer truth','Eliminate all parser errors','Make every old field safe','Schema changes affect consuming code and need an intentional compatibility policy.','Shape versions do not prove facts.','Malformed responses can still occur.','Old fields can have incompatible meaning.'],
['A response reaches a limit halfway through a JSON object. What should the app avoid?','Treating it as a completed result','Recording the incomplete state','Using a bounded recovery policy','Explaining that no result completed','Partial output must not be promoted into a finished business decision.','State recording is useful.','Recovery may be appropriate.','Truthful feedback helps users.'],
['A refusal arrives where an invoice object was expected. What would be unsafe?','Inventing an approved invoice','Showing the refusal outcome','Retaining safe correlation data','Following the documented adapter path','Fabricating a business object misrepresents the response and can trigger unauthorized work.','A distinct refusal path is appropriate.','Safe diagnostics support repair.','The actual contract should govern.'],
['A schema changes category meaning while keeping the same key. What needs review?','Semantic compatibility','Only key sorting','Only response length','Only font weight','Compatibility includes field meaning, not just unchanged syntax.','Ordering does not preserve meaning.','Length does not define semantics.','Typography is unrelated.'],
['A parser retries invalid output without any attempt or deadline bound. What is missing?','A recovery budget','A different assistant avatar','A longer schema title','A public API key','Repair/retry policies must bound repeated work and respect failure categories.','Avatars do not limit attempts.','Titles do not control elapsed time.','Credential exposure removes control.'],
['The fixture routes state complete to validate_payload. What does that imply?','Further validation is required','The business action already ran','All facts are verified','The schema can be skipped','A complete envelope permits validation; it does not certify the payload or authorize execution.','No mutation is performed.','Facts are not checked by routing.','Schema checks remain necessary.']
]},
{slug:'evaluation-set',comp:'m3.c1',title:'Build a fixed prompt-evaluation set with meaningful cases',before:'Review independent evaluation and task contracts. Generated drafts remain subject to human review.',intro:'A prompt change improves one impressive support example. Before adopting it, the team needs a stable set of cases that represent normal work, important boundaries and failures.',sections:[S('Define the evaluation unit','Each case needs a stable identifier, task input, relevant evidence and expected properties or a reference outcome. Include straightforward cases, ambiguous requests, missing evidence, schema edge cases and critical authorization or policy boundaries. Avoid recording sensitive real data when synthetic cases can exercise the requirement.','A fixed set supports paired comparison: each prompt version sees the same cases under declared model/settings. Record all versions and use a held-out set for final decisions when repeated development against the main set risks overfitting.'),S('Check data quality before scoring','Duplicate or conflicting cases can distort results; a large count of name-swapped examples is not meaningful breadth. Inspect coverage by task requirement and relevant segment rather than only total sample count. Label uncertainty and unresolved rubric disagreements.','The fixture checks unique synthetic case ids and summarizes explicit categories. It is not semantic deduplication, a representative sampling proof or a model evaluation run; those require additional design and evidence.')],problem:'Audit a synthetic evaluation set for id uniqueness and boundary-case coverage.',code:'cases = [{"id": "e1", "kind": "normal"}, {"id": "e2", "kind": "missing_evidence"}, {"id": "e3", "kind": "schema_edge"}, {"id": "e4", "kind": "access_boundary"}]\nids = [c["id"] for c in cases]\nprint(len(ids) == len(set(ids)))\nprint(sorted({c["kind"] for c in cases}))\nprint(all("id" in c and "kind" in c for c in cases))',expect:"True\n['access_boundary', 'missing_evidence', 'normal', 'schema_edge']\nTrue",steps:['Give each synthetic case an explicit id and category.','Check exact id uniqueness.','Inspect the declared coverage categories before scoring.'],limit:'Unique ids and category labels do not prove semantic diversity, correct expected answers, representativeness or any model’s performance.',mistakes:['Judging a new prompt from one cherry-picked success.','Counting duplicate/name-swapped cases as broad coverage.'],takeaways:['Use fixed cases with traceable expected properties.','Inspect coverage and label quality before interpreting scores.'],refs:['evals'],questions:[
['Why use a fixed case set to compare prompt versions?','Control the task comparison','Guarantee the same output text','Eliminate every future failure','Avoid recording model versions','Paired cases make differences more interpretable than unrelated demonstrations.','Generation can still vary.','Finite tests cannot prove all futures.','Versions remain important metadata.'],
['What should an evaluation case contain besides an input?','Expected properties or outcome','Only a random display color','Only a customer nickname','Only the prompt author’s praise','A rubric or reference defines what successful task behavior means.','Color does not define correctness.','Names do not establish acceptance.','Praise is not a test oracle.'],
['Do unique case ids prove semantically diverse coverage?','No, content may duplicate','Yes, ids prove all meaning','Yes, after sorting the list','Yes, with a larger model','Different ids can refer to equivalent or repeated cases.','Identifiers do not encode full meaning.','Sorting does not add diversity.','Model size does not change dataset duplication.'],
['A prompt improves one public demo but fails missing-evidence cases. What should the report include?','The relevant failure segment','Only the polished demo','Only total prompt length','Only the newest model logo','Evaluation should expose important task boundaries rather than cherry-pick visible successes.','One demo hides the regression.','Length is not outcome evidence.','Branding does not measure cases.'],
['An evaluation set includes actual private student records unnecessarily. What should be reviewed?','Authorized minimal data','Only case-id formatting','Only response punctuation','Only the chart’s colors','Test design should use appropriate authorized data and synthetic fixtures where sufficient.','Ids do not authorize records.','Punctuation is unrelated.','Visual design is not privacy control.'],
['Developers repeatedly optimize against the same evaluation answers. What additional set can support a final estimate?','An independent holdout','A renamed copy of the same answers','Only more easy duplicates','Only the training prompts','A holdout should remain separate from the development choices it evaluates.','Renaming does not restore independence.','Easy repeats do not add breadth.','Development prompts are not independent evidence.'],
['The fixture lists an access_boundary category. What remains unproved?','Meaningful access coverage','The string category exists','The id uniqueness check ran','The list has four entries','A label is metadata; meaningful case design and assertions still need review.','The field is explicit.','The check is executed.','The entries are directly countable.'],
['A team adds twenty name-swapped versions of one easy case. What should coverage review prioritize?','Distinct task boundaries','Only the larger raw count','Only alphabetic sorting','Only shorter case identifiers','Meaningful coverage comes from different task conditions and failure modes rather than cosmetic variation.','Counts can be inflated by duplicates.','Sorting does not change semantics.','Ids do not establish task breadth.']
]},
{slug:'scoring',comp:'m3.c1',title:'Score task properties and inspect paired changes',before:'Use fixed case identifiers and a declared rubric. Distinguish syntax, semantics and critical failures.',intro:'Two prompt versions both produce valid JSON, but one mislabels urgent support cases. A score that checks only parsing would miss the product’s most important regression.',sections:[S('Choose a scorer aligned with the requirement','Deterministic code can check exact labels, required fields, numerical relationships and other well-defined properties. Open-ended qualities may need a reviewed rubric and human judgment or a separately validated model-based judge. A judge’s fluency does not make it unbiased or authoritative.','Do not collapse every property into one opaque score. Report format validity, task correctness, evidence support and critical failures separately where those dimensions matter. Treat scorer changes as versioned system changes too.'),S('Use paired outcomes to find tradeoffs','Compare the same case under old and new prompts. Count improvements, regressions, unchanged successes and unchanged failures; inspect the actual cases behind each count. A higher average can coexist with a critical regression.','The fixture compares synthetic boolean outcomes on three matching cases. It demonstrates paired accounting, not real model performance, statistical significance or the validity of any particular rubric.')],problem:'Count improvements and regressions from paired synthetic outcomes.',code:'old = {"normal": True, "missing": False, "critical": True}\nnew = {"normal": True, "missing": True, "critical": False}\nimproved = [k for k in old if not old[k] and new[k]]\nregressed = [k for k in old if old[k] and not new[k]]\nprint(improved)\nprint(regressed)\nprint(sum(old.values()), sum(new.values()))',expect:"['missing']\n['critical']\n2 2",steps:['Align outcomes by stable case id.','Identify cases that improve and regress.','Compare totals without hiding the critical changed case.'],limit:'Invented booleans only demonstrate paired counts. The fixture does not validate a scorer, call a model or establish statistical confidence about real prompt performance.',mistakes:['Using JSON parse success as the only task-quality score.','Accepting an aggregate gain without reviewing critical regressions.'],takeaways:['Score the properties that matter to the product.','Inspect paired case changes as well as totals.'],refs:['evals','structure'],questions:[
['Why is JSON parsing alone insufficient for a support classifier evaluation?','Labels can still be wrong','Parsing proves all semantics','Schemas grant user permission','Every valid object is useful','Syntax checks do not establish correct classification or business meaning.','Semantics require task checks.','Permissions remain external policy.','A valid representation can be unsuitable.'],
['What does paired evaluation compare?','The same cases across versions','Unrelated handpicked demos','Only the prompt word counts','Only model marketing names','Stable case alignment helps identify which outcomes changed under a new version.','Different cases confound the comparison.','Length does not measure task success.','Names are not outcomes.'],
['Why version the scorer?','Its rules affect reported results','It automatically trains the model','It grants production access','It removes all judge bias','A changed rubric or checking function can change scores independently of the prompt.','Scoring need not train weights.','Access is a separate workflow.','Bias still needs evaluation.'],
['A prompt gains one normal case and loses a critical access-boundary case. What should a reviewer inspect?','The critical regression','Only the unchanged total','Only the answer length','Only the UI response color','Material safety or policy failures cannot be hidden by an unchanged or improved aggregate.','Totals can conceal changed failures.','Length is not the boundary criterion.','Color does not establish quality.'],
['A model-based judge gives high scores to verbose answers. What should be validated?','Judge bias and rubric alignment','Only the judge’s confidence','Only the model’s popularity','Only the number of adjectives','A judge is itself a system with possible biases and needs evidence that it measures the intended property.','Confidence is not calibration.','Popularity does not establish validity.','Style counts are not necessarily task quality.'],
['A numerical extraction task has a known exact total. What scorer can directly check it?','A deterministic invariant','Only a style preference','Only a fluent explanation','Only the answer’s token count','Well-defined numeric relationships can be checked directly under the declared domain rules.','Style does not establish arithmetic.','Prose can be plausible but wrong.','Length is not the numeric relation.'],
['The fixture’s totals are equal while one case improves and another regresses. What does that show?','Totals can hide tradeoffs','The prompts are fully equivalent','The critical case is irrelevant','The scorer proved real model quality','Aggregates can conceal materially different case outcomes.','Equal counts do not imply equal behavior.','Criticality is a task requirement.','The data are synthetic.'],
['A release changes both prompt and scorer. What should the analysis separate?','Behavior and measurement changes','Only the report title','Only response punctuation','Only the user’s browser version','Without controlling scorer changes, a score difference may not reflect only model/prompt behavior.','Titles do not explain confounding.','Punctuation is not the entire metric.','Browser version is not the stated change.']
]},
{slug:'regression-gates',comp:'m3.c1',title:'Turn prompt evaluation into a recoverable release gate',before:'Use paired scoring, fixed cases and critical-segment reporting. Review draft versus human approval status.',intro:'A team wants to ship a shorter prompt for a SaaS help center. The new version saves tokens, but the release decision must preserve task quality, critical boundaries and a clear rollback path.',sections:[S('Define acceptance before seeing the results','Specify minimum task scores, critical-case rules, latency/cost bounds and evidence requirements before comparing candidates. Otherwise it is easy to rationalize whichever result looks attractive. Keep automated technical validation distinct from human review and deployment approval.','Record prompt, schema, model, adapter, scorer and dataset versions with the result. Failed gates should quarantine the candidate while preserving the last accepted version and its history. Do not overwrite a human-edited prompt or approval record silently.'),S('Monitor the real system after a controlled release','Offline cases are important but cannot cover every production input or provider change. Use safe operational metrics and sampled review under data-access policy, then add meaningful regression cases for confirmed failures. Avoid indiscriminate private prompt logging.','A rollback plan should identify the previous known version and the state needed to restore it without erasing user history. The fixture evaluates synthetic gate conditions only; it does not deploy, approve content or demonstrate production reliability.')],problem:'Apply a synthetic quality and critical-failure gate to two candidate reports.',code:'def gate(report):\n    return report["task_score"] >= 0.90 and report["critical_failures"] == 0 and report["schema_failures"] == 0\nreports = [{"task_score": 0.95, "critical_failures": 1, "schema_failures": 0}, {"task_score": 0.92, "critical_failures": 0, "schema_failures": 0}]\nprint([gate(r) for r in reports])\nprint("human approval remains separate")',expect:'[False, True]\nhuman approval remains separate',steps:['Declare the synthetic gate before evaluating reports.','Apply both aggregate and critical conditions.','Keep technical acceptance separate from human approval.'],limit:'These invented thresholds are not universal product standards. Passing the local predicate does not establish actual model quality, human review, deployment authorization or production outcomes.',mistakes:['Changing acceptance criteria after seeing a favored candidate’s failures.','Treating an automated pass as human approval or a deployment command.'],takeaways:['Version evidence and quarantine failed candidates.','Preserve prior releases and review/deployment authority.'],refs:['evals','structure'],questions:[
['When should a release’s acceptance criteria be defined?','Before choosing the winning result','Only after reading every score','Only after a deployment fails','Only when the UI looks polished','Predeclared criteria reduce post-hoc rationalization and make comparisons reviewable.','Changing rules after results can bias decisions.','Waiting for failure misses the gate.','Appearance does not define acceptance.'],
['Does an automated evaluation pass constitute human approval?','No, keep the gates separate','Yes, by definition','Yes, if the score is high','Yes, when logs are saved','Technical checks and human authorization/review are distinct workflow states.','Automation does not create human review.','Scores do not change authority.','Logs are evidence, not approval.'],
['What should a failed candidate leave intact?','The prior accepted version','Only its own polished screenshot','A rewritten user history','A fabricated approval record','Quarantining a failure should preserve recoverable prior behavior and historical evidence.','Screenshots are not a rollback state.','History must not be rewritten.','Approvals cannot be invented.'],
['A new prompt has a high mean but one critical failure. What should a zero-critical-failure gate do?','Reject the candidate','Average away the failure','Publish it automatically','Erase the critical case','A hard critical condition must be enforced even when the aggregate looks good.','Averages do not override the declared rule.','Publication requires separate authority.','Removing tests hides evidence.'],
['A provider changes model behavior after offline tests passed. What should operations support?','Monitoring and regression updates','A promise of permanent correctness','Deletion of old version metadata','Unlimited private prompt logging','Real systems need safe observation, confirmed-failure cases and recoverable versioning.','Offline success is not a permanent guarantee.','Metadata helps diagnosis.','Monitoring should minimize private data.'],
['A rollback restores an old prompt but erases users’ newer conversation history. What constraint was violated?','Preservation of user records','Only the output schema title','Only token formatting','Only the dashboard icon order','Recovering application behavior must not silently destroy unrelated user data or evidence.','Titles do not protect history.','Formatting is not persistence.','Icons are unrelated.'],
['A report stores exact prompt, model, schema, dataset and scorer versions. What does this support?','Traceable evaluation evidence','Universal deterministic output','Automatic legal compliance','All future task coverage','Version metadata helps reproduce and interpret conditions without guaranteeing every broader property.','Stochastic outputs can still vary.','Metadata alone is not compliance.','Finite cases cannot cover all future inputs.'],
['The gate fixture returns true for a report. Which conclusion is accurate?','Declared local conditions passed','The content is human reviewed','The application was deployed','Every future response is safe','A predicate result applies to its supplied data and conditions, not unperformed review or deployment.','No human action is demonstrated.','No deployment occurs.','Future behavior is unmeasured.']
]}
